            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Visual Studio Parallel Stacks</h2>
                    <p>Debug &gt; Windows &gt; Parallel Stacks (Ctrl+Shift+D, S)</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>View all threads and their call stacks</li>
                        <li>Toggle "Tasks" view for async operations</li>
                        <li>See waiting, running, and scheduled tasks</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Tasks Window</h2>
                    <p>Example output from the Tasks window showing task IDs, status, and description - useful to identify active vs waiting tasks while debugging.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-text">ID    Status          Description
1     Waiting         GetDataAsync
2     Running         ProcessItemAsync  
3     Scheduled       SaveResultAsync</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Async Stack Traces</h2>
                    <p>Enable full debug symbols or use tools like Ben.Demystifier to get clearer async stack traces that show the logical call path across awaits.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Enable better async stack traces
&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;DebugType&gt;full&lt;/DebugType&gt;
&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;

// Or use Ben.Demystifier package
try
{
    await DoWorkAsync();
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    Console.WriteLine(ex.Demystify());
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Detecting Thread Pool Starvation</h2>
                    <p>Collect ThreadPool metrics such as available threads vs max to detect starvation; low available threads and timeouts indicate pressure.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Monitor thread pool
ThreadPool.GetMinThreads(out int workerMin, out int ioMin);
ThreadPool.GetMaxThreads(out int workerMax, out int ioMax);
ThreadPool.GetAvailableThreads(out int workerAvail, out int ioAvail);

Console.WriteLine($"Workers: {workerMax - workerAvail}/{workerMax}");

// Signs of starvation:
// - Available threads near 0
// - Requests timing out
// - High CPU with low actual work</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>dotnet-counters</h2>
                    <p>Commands to install and monitor runtime counters like thread pool and queue lengths; useful for live diagnostics during load testing.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Install
dotnet tool install -g dotnet-counters

# Monitor async metrics
dotnet counters monitor --process-id &lt;pid&gt; \
    --counters System.Runtime,Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting

# Key counters:
# - threadpool-thread-count
# - threadpool-queue-length
# - active-timer-count</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>dotnet-trace for Profiling</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Collect trace
dotnet trace collect --process-id &lt;pid&gt; --duration 00:00:30

# Analyze with:
# - PerfView (Windows)
# - Speedscope (cross-platform, web)
# - Visual Studio</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use Parallel Stacks and Tasks windows in VS.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>dotnet-counters for runtime metrics.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>dotnet-trace for detailed profiling.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Watch for thread pool starvation signs.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>

